1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:02,510
That gray suggestion is a proposal.

2
00:00:03,030 --> 00:00:05,190
Before it becomes your code, you get a choice.

3
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:08,150
Take it, take a piece, or dismiss it.

4
00:00:08,570 --> 00:00:10,710
Let's make those choices deliberately in Cursor.

5
00:00:11,230 --> 00:00:13,470
We'll turn a tiny status helper into tested

6
00:00:13,470 --> 00:00:16,970
behavior using real Tab suggestions, and we'll catch

7
00:00:16,970 --> 00:00:19,410
a setting that makes the partial acceptance shortcut

8
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:20,710
behave very differently.

9
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:22,670
Fast typing is nice.

10
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:25,450
Knowing what you just accepted is better.

11
00:00:27,883 --> 00:00:29,423
Here's our practice helper.

12
00:00:29,883 --> 00:00:32,803
An open request should display Awaiting review.

13
00:00:33,343 --> 00:00:35,603
An approved request should display Approved.

14
00:00:36,363 --> 00:00:39,783
Every other exact string
should display Unknown status.

15
00:00:40,263 --> 00:00:43,403
No trimming, no changing the
case, no clever guessing.

16
00:00:43,883 --> 00:00:46,183
The starter just returns its input, so it

17
00:00:46,183 --> 00:00:47,603
doesn't meet that contract yet.

18
00:00:48,403 --> 00:00:52,023
These comments and the tests
are prepared exercise material.

19
00:00:52,023 --> 00:00:55,063
The suggestions we're about to inspect come from

20
00:00:55,063 --> 00:00:55,883
Cursor Tab.

21
00:00:58,383 --> 00:01:01,463
The tests cover both supported values and a

22
00:01:01,463 --> 00:01:03,203
group of unrecognized strings.

23
00:01:03,923 --> 00:01:07,123
That group includes an empty string, uppercase OPEN,

24
00:01:07,583 --> 00:01:11,223
a value padded with spaces, and other unknown values.

25
00:01:11,863 --> 00:01:13,843
Why be this explicit?

26
00:01:14,483 --> 00:01:18,663
Because a completion can look
perfectly reasonable while

27
00:01:18,663 --> 00:01:20,763
quietly changing your requirements.

28
00:01:21,683 --> 00:01:25,023
These checks give us a
concrete target before we edit.

29
00:01:25,543 --> 00:01:26,983
They're focused on this helper.

30
00:01:26,983 --> 00:01:30,543
They don't exercise a live
interface or the whole application.

31
00:01:33,183 --> 00:01:34,983
In Cursor Settings, open Tab.

32
00:01:35,583 --> 00:01:38,603
This installation has a separate
switch for partial acceptance.

33
00:01:39,243 --> 00:01:41,603
Turn it on to accept a suggestion incrementally

34
00:01:41,603 --> 00:01:43,783
with Control, Right Arrow on Linux.

35
00:01:44,483 --> 00:01:47,063
During rehearsal, with the switch off, that key

36
00:01:47,063 --> 00:01:48,303
moved the caret instead.

37
00:01:48,863 --> 00:01:50,743
So if your shortcut doesn't do what you

38
00:01:50,743 --> 00:01:53,663
expect, inspect the setting
and the installed binding.

39
00:01:54,303 --> 00:01:56,683
Don't keep pressing keys and hope the editor

40
00:01:56,683 --> 00:01:57,563
reads your mind.

41
00:02:00,083 --> 00:02:03,023
Select the starter return line and begin an

42
00:02:03,023 --> 00:02:04,923
if condition that compares status.

43
00:02:05,723 --> 00:02:07,363
Pause after the comparison operator.

44
00:02:08,163 --> 00:02:10,843
The editor offers gray text that completes the

45
00:02:10,843 --> 00:02:11,783
condition for open.

46
00:02:12,463 --> 00:02:15,723
Read the proposal and the surrounding code
before accepting it.

47
00:02:16,203 --> 00:02:17,983
The gray portion is still a suggestion.

48
00:02:18,823 --> 00:02:21,483
You've already changed the file by typing, but

49
00:02:21,483 --> 00:02:23,183
you haven't accepted the gray text.

50
00:02:23,723 --> 00:02:24,983
Keep those two states separate.

51
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:29,310
Press Escape.

52
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:33,130
The suggestion disappears while
the condition we typed remains.

53
00:02:33,830 --> 00:02:36,170
That's a rejection of the proposal, not an

54
00:02:36,170 --> 00:02:37,430
undo of our own edit.

55
00:02:38,110 --> 00:02:41,010
You can dismiss a perfectly
sensible suggestion because

56
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:42,210
you're not ready to use it.

57
00:02:42,770 --> 00:02:45,030
You can also continue typing your intended code.

58
00:02:45,730 --> 00:02:47,910
The useful habit is to keep control of

59
00:02:47,910 --> 00:02:50,490
the next edit instead of treating every gray

60
00:02:50,490 --> 00:02:51,510
line as an instruction.

61
00:02:54,050 --> 00:02:57,530
Here we have a fresh suggestion ready to inspect.

62
00:02:58,190 --> 00:03:02,170
With Partial Accepts enabled,
use Control, Right Arrow,

63
00:03:02,550 --> 00:03:05,530
then pause and inspect what became real text.

64
00:03:06,330 --> 00:03:08,090
Repeat once to accept another piece.

65
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:10,830
In this example, the quote and the word

66
00:03:10,830 --> 00:03:11,970
are separate steps.

67
00:03:12,730 --> 00:03:14,470
Punctuation boundaries can be surprising.

68
00:03:15,350 --> 00:03:17,210
Watch the caret and the text color.

69
00:03:17,610 --> 00:03:20,950
Don't assume one key press always
inserts a whole expression.

70
00:03:23,517 --> 00:03:26,777
Now, press Tab to accept the
remainder of the condition.

71
00:03:27,697 --> 00:03:30,157
Cursor then offers the body as another proposal.

72
00:03:30,817 --> 00:03:31,337
Stop again.

73
00:03:31,937 --> 00:03:34,277
We need the review label for open, the

74
00:03:34,277 --> 00:03:38,137
approved label for approved, and
an explicit unknown fallback.

75
00:03:38,817 --> 00:03:40,937
A second suggestion is a second decision.

76
00:03:41,637 --> 00:03:44,977
Accepting the condition didn't
automatically approve every later

77
00:03:44,977 --> 00:03:46,237
line the model might offer.

78
00:03:48,717 --> 00:03:52,177
This proposal follows our three outcomes and uses

79
00:03:52,177 --> 00:03:53,517
exact comparisons.

80
00:03:54,157 --> 00:03:55,897
Accept it, then save the file.

81
00:03:56,517 --> 00:03:58,937
The gray code becomes ordinary source text.

82
00:03:59,557 --> 00:04:01,897
That change of color tells us it was inserted.

83
00:04:02,417 --> 00:04:04,337
It doesn't tell us the behavior is correct.

84
00:04:04,977 --> 00:04:07,897
We'll inspect the actual
diff and run the checks next.

85
00:04:08,697 --> 00:04:11,037
Your completion may be shaped differently.

86
00:04:11,617 --> 00:04:14,597
Evaluate it against the same contract instead of

87
00:04:14,597 --> 00:04:16,277
chasing identical ghost text.

88
00:04:18,817 --> 00:04:21,337
Open the diff and compare the helper with

89
00:04:21,337 --> 00:04:22,257
its starting version.

90
00:04:22,857 --> 00:04:25,537
The raw return has become explicit branches.

91
00:04:26,417 --> 00:04:29,137
Check the returned strings, the fallback, and the

92
00:04:29,137 --> 00:04:29,937
scope of the edit.

93
00:04:30,617 --> 00:04:32,557
Only this source file should need to change

94
00:04:32,557 --> 00:04:33,377
for the exercise.

95
00:04:34,137 --> 00:04:37,077
Formatting deserves attention
too, but a beautifully aligned

96
00:04:37,077 --> 00:04:38,897
wrong label is still wrong.

97
00:04:39,717 --> 00:04:43,177
Review behavior first, then keep the code consistent

98
00:04:43,177 --> 00:04:44,617
with the surrounding project.

99
00:04:47,150 --> 00:04:50,890
Run the focused status tests,
then the TypeScript check.

100
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:53,890
We want a successful exit from both commands,

101
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:56,370
and we want to read what actually ran.

102
00:04:57,030 --> 00:04:59,490
The tests check the supported labels and the

103
00:04:59,490 --> 00:05:00,470
unknown values.

104
00:05:00,990 --> 00:05:03,650
The type check catches a different class of problems.

105
00:05:04,170 --> 00:05:07,010
Together, they provide evidence
for this bounded change.

106
00:05:07,410 --> 00:05:10,250
They aren't a certificate that every possible input

107
00:05:10,250 --> 00:05:12,250
or the whole product has been verified.

108
00:05:14,617 --> 00:05:17,017
Here's an original course exercise.

109
00:05:17,917 --> 00:05:20,217
Choose an input and compare an exact mapping

110
00:05:20,217 --> 00:05:22,657
with a version that trims and lower cases first.

111
00:05:23,357 --> 00:05:25,017
For open both may look fine.

112
00:05:25,497 --> 00:05:28,757
For uppercase OPEN or a padded value, they disagree.

113
00:05:29,497 --> 00:05:31,797
Our contract says those values are unknown.

114
00:05:32,857 --> 00:05:35,617
Normalization can be a useful feature, but adding

115
00:05:35,617 --> 00:05:37,117
it here would change the requirement.

116
00:05:37,117 --> 00:05:40,957
A helpful-looking completion
still needs that conversation.

117
00:05:43,583 --> 00:05:44,403
Checkpoint.

118
00:05:45,063 --> 00:05:47,963
A suggestion adds normalization we didn't request.

119
00:05:48,683 --> 00:05:49,403
What should you do?

120
00:05:50,183 --> 00:05:53,183
Reject or revise that part, then verify the

121
00:05:53,183 --> 00:05:54,063
intended behavior.

122
00:05:55,043 --> 00:05:57,563
Now, suppose the condition is useful, but you're

123
00:05:57,563 --> 00:05:58,763
still reviewing the rest.

124
00:05:59,763 --> 00:06:02,123
Partial acceptance can help you take a smaller step.

125
00:06:02,643 --> 00:06:04,583
It doesn't remove the need to read the

126
00:06:04,583 --> 00:06:07,503
remaining proposal, and it doesn't make an incomplete

127
00:06:07,503 --> 00:06:09,423
intermediate state ready to commit.

128
00:06:11,917 --> 00:06:14,937
The Cursor Tab status control lets you inspect

129
00:06:14,937 --> 00:06:17,437
whether suggestions are enabled and gives you ways

130
00:06:17,437 --> 00:06:18,237
to pause them.

131
00:06:18,617 --> 00:06:20,797
Use that when you need a quiet editing moment.

132
00:06:21,577 --> 00:06:24,897
Tab can also suggest larger edits
or another editing location.

133
00:06:25,457 --> 00:06:27,657
We aren't depending on those
features in this exercise.

134
00:06:28,537 --> 00:06:30,797
Read the current suggestion and its scope each

135
00:06:30,797 --> 00:06:33,677
time and inspect your installed controls when their

136
00:06:33,677 --> 00:06:35,177
behavior differs from the reference.

137
00:06:37,683 --> 00:06:41,023
Keep a small acceptance record, the behavior you

138
00:06:41,023 --> 00:06:43,783
wanted, the file you changed, the part you

139
00:06:43,783 --> 00:06:46,603
accepted or rejected, and the checks you ran.

140
00:06:47,163 --> 00:06:49,483
Include the revision and the actual result.

141
00:06:50,103 --> 00:06:52,423
This isn't a form you need for every keystroke.

142
00:06:52,863 --> 00:06:55,323
It's practice in making your reasoning visible.

143
00:06:56,143 --> 00:06:58,903
When a teammate reviews the change, the contract

144
00:06:58,903 --> 00:07:01,423
and the evidence matter far more than how

145
00:07:01,423 --> 00:07:02,823
many suggestions you accepted.

146
00:07:05,383 --> 00:07:08,503
For your exercise, start from the supplied practice

147
00:07:08,503 --> 00:07:12,643
snapshot and define one additional
status before asking for a completion.

148
00:07:13,363 --> 00:07:16,283
Write its expected label and add a focused test.

149
00:07:17,043 --> 00:07:20,983
Inspect whatever Tab actually
suggests, accept deliberately, and

150
00:07:20,983 --> 00:07:21,863
verify the result.

151
00:07:22,443 --> 00:07:25,223
If it adds unrelated behavior, remove that behavior

152
00:07:25,223 --> 00:07:26,503
or reject the proposal.

153
00:07:27,163 --> 00:07:29,743
Keep a before and after diff and explain

154
00:07:29,743 --> 00:07:32,523
one decision you made instead of simply pressing Tab.

155
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:38,610
You now have three useful moves, accept the

156
00:07:38,610 --> 00:07:41,910
whole proposal, accept a smaller piece, and dismiss

157
00:07:41,910 --> 00:07:43,550
it while keeping your own typing.

158
00:07:44,250 --> 00:07:46,930
We used those moves inside a loop, define

159
00:07:46,930 --> 00:07:50,530
the behavior, inspect the change,
and run the relevant checks.

160
00:07:51,210 --> 00:07:53,930
That's how autocomplete becomes an engineering tool.

161
00:07:54,530 --> 00:07:56,890
Before the next lesson, what extra behavior would

162
00:07:56,890 --> 00:07:59,370
make you reject a suggestion that otherwise looked

163
00:07:59,370 --> 00:08:00,130
pretty good?
